print_session_information() {
    cat <<'EOF'

    Select "Aqueous" from your display manager's Wayland session list.
    The session runs through uwsm, which manages the compositor lifecycle,
    exports the live Wayland/XWayland environment, starts graphical-session
    services, and stops them cleanly at logout. No Aqueous user unit needs to
    be enabled manually.

    User configuration lives in ~/.config/aqueous/. On first login, wm.toml
    and the inert outputs.toml template are copied there only when missing.
    Aqueous also supports separate layout.toml, input.toml, and rules.toml
    files and hot-reloads valid changes without restarting the compositor.

    Open the standalone Aqueous Settings application from the application
    menu or run aqueous-settings to edit your compositor configuration.

    Useful commands:
        aqueous-settings
        aqueousctl windows
        aqueousctl outputs
        aqueousctl layout --output <name> --json

    Session log:
        $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/aqueous-wm.log

    Screen recording and sharing use the packaged Aqueous portal backend;
    xdg-desktop-portal-wlr does not need to be installed separately.
    The source picker uses the shell selected by your Aqueous package.
    Existing user portal configs override the packaged chooser settings.

    SDDM and other Wayland-capable display managers also work.
EOF

    if [ -e /usr/lib/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/dms.service ]; then
        cat <<'EOF'

    DankMaterialShell (dms-shell) starts automatically with the Aqueous session.
    Use DMS Settings for shell appearance. Aqueous Screen Sharing is a separate
    plugin, enabled on first use; an intentional disable is preserved.
    The optional greetd-dms-greeter-bin provides a display-manager frontend.

    Existing user configuration is preserved. When switching shells, merge
    the bindings from /usr/share/aqueous/wm.toml into ~/.config/aqueous/wm.toml:
        launcher: dms ipc call spotlight toggle
        screenshots (including direct bindings): dms screenshot region
    dms.service is enabled by the package; no manual enable command is needed.
    Disable any manually enabled noctalia.service, aqueous-dms.service, or
    duplicate shell startup command so that only dms.service starts the shell.
EOF
    fi
}

post_install() {
    printf '==> Aqueous %s installed.\n' "${1:-unknown}"
    print_session_information
}

post_upgrade() {
    printf '==> Aqueous upgraded from %s to %s.\n' "${2:-unknown}" "${1:-unknown}"
    cat <<'EOF'

    Restart the Aqueous session to load the new compositor, bundled wlroots,
    Aqueous portal backend, environment, and user units. Existing files in
    ~/.config/aqueous are not overwritten by the session launcher. Review any
    pacnew files created for system-wide configuration before merging them.

    The old DMS aqueousSettings and Noctalia aqueous/settings plugins are
    replaced by the standalone aqueous-settings application. Remove their
    widgets from custom bars and change old panel/IPC launch bindings to run
    aqueous-settings. User settings and backups are kept.
    The optional DMS aqueousSettingsAppearance typography bridge remains.

    If upgrading while logged in, refresh the user-unit cache first:
        systemctl --user daemon-reload
EOF
    print_session_information
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'EOF'
==> Aqueous removed.

    Per-user configuration, cache, and state were retained:
        ~/.config/aqueous
        ~/.cache/aqueous
        ~/.local/state/aqueous

    Remove those directories manually if they are no longer wanted.
EOF
}
